WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:04.149
A lot of folks are understandably ready to
pull the plug on 2016.

00:00:04.149 --> 00:00:05.989
But before you pop the champagne,

00:00:05.989 --> 00:00:09.829
here are 5 things Libertarians should be nervous about in the new year.

00:00:11.990 --> 00:00:14.950
At a time when most Americans support legalization,

00:00:14.950 --> 00:00:18.900
2017 could be bad news for those in favor of legal weed.

00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:21.940
Though Trump has said legalization should
be up to the states,

00:00:21.940 --> 00:00:26.180
he's been appointing anti-marijuana law makers to key positions in his cabinet.

00:00:26.180 --> 00:00:31.600
Most notable of these is Alabama senator Jeff
Sessions who's been tapped as Attorney General.

00:00:31.610 --> 00:00:35.940
Sessions has long opposed legalization and
has criticized both the Obama administration

00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:39.850
and the Department of Justice for not enforcing
federal marijuana laws.

00:00:39.850 --> 00:00:43.130
I mean we need grown-ups in charge in Washington

00:00:43.130 --> 00:00:47.350
to say marijuana is not the kind of thing that ought to be legalized.

00:00:49.450 --> 00:00:51.270
When Donald Trump is sworn into office,

00:00:51.270 --> 00:00:55.200
he'll be inheriting a debt that has nearly doubled under President Obama.

00:00:55.200 --> 00:01:00.370
In early 2017 the national debt is set to
hit a staggering 20 trillion dollars.

00:01:00.370 --> 00:01:05.089
But don't expect the new administration to
make America's fiscal nightmare great again.

00:01:05.089 --> 00:01:07.369
Estimates from the Committee for a Responsible Budget

00:01:07.369 --> 00:01:13.669
say Trump's policy proposals will add another 5.3 trillion dollars over the next four years.

00:01:15.660 --> 00:01:19.040
Globalization and free trade have lifted millions
out of poverty

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:21.960
and raised living standards across the United States.

00:01:21.970 --> 00:01:25.670
Yet we enter 2017 with both Democrats and
Republicans

00:01:25.670 --> 00:01:28.480
questioning the fundamental value of free markets.

00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:32.680
A new administration promising to inject itself
into the affairs of private companies

00:01:32.680 --> 00:01:37.080
and a President openly threatening a costly trade
war with China.

00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:45.060
It's now clear that so-called fake news can
have real world consequences.

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:50.320
Those consequences now include Facebook now
testing new plugins to limit misinformation

00:01:50.329 --> 00:01:54.389
and partnering with fact checking groups to
root out any false news items,

00:01:54.389 --> 00:01:58.159
causing fears of censorship on the popular social media platform.

00:01:58.159 --> 00:02:02.779
When policy makers and politicians start pressuring
private companies to limit speech,

00:02:02.780 --> 00:02:03.940
expect trouble.

00:02:06.280 --> 00:02:11.560
The FBI, NSA and CIA are most likely going
to get shiny new surveillance powers

00:02:11.560 --> 00:02:13.760
under a Trump administration.

00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:16.170
Now some of these changes are already happening.

00:02:16.170 --> 00:02:20.230
A new rule approved this fall allows federal
agents with a single search warrant

00:02:20.230 --> 00:02:23.780
to hack millions of American's computers or smart
phones at once.

00:02:23.780 --> 00:02:29.360
And it's not just Uncle Sam, the United Kingdom
just approved the Investigatory Powers bill,

00:02:29.370 --> 00:02:32.590
which gives the UK's global surveillance program
authorities power

00:02:32.590 --> 00:02:34.580
to create a new government database

00:02:34.580 --> 00:02:38.780
that will store the web history of
every citizen in the country.

00:02:38.790 --> 00:02:43.150
So by all means say goodbye to 2016, but don't
let your guard down,

00:02:43.150 --> 00:02:45.740
there'll be plenty to be nervous about in the new year.

